<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01900</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>263011bc-0e72-433c-9337-833eade7c58c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>A quadratic function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=(x-7)(x+3)\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Without graphing, identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of \(f\).
      <list>
        <item> \((7,0),(-3,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((-7,0),(3,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,7),(0,-3)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,-7),(0,3)\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((7,0),(-3,0)\). If \(x\) is 7, \((7-7)(7+3)\) gives (0,10), which is 0.   If \(x\) is -3, \((-3-7)(-3+3)\) gives (-10,0), which is 0.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Use the standard form of \((x-7)(x+3)\) to identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\).  </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> – 21</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer these questions for the equation \(y=x^2-5x+4\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of this equation’s graph? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> An equivalent way to write this equation is \(y=(x-4)(x-1)\). What are the \(x\)-intercepts of this equation’s graph?
      <list>
        <item> \((1,0),(4,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((-1,0),(-4,0)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,1),(0,4)\) </item>
        <item> \((0,-1),(0,-4)\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((1,0),(4,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Noah said that if we graph \(y=(x-1)(x+6)\), the \(x\)-intercepts will be at \((1,0)\) and \((-6,0)\). Is Noah correct?
    <list>
      <item> Yes </item>
      <item> No </item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes. We can check by substituting 1 for \(x\) and -6 for \(x\) and see if they produce an output of 0. If \(x\) is 1, \((1-1)(1+6)\) gives (0)(7), which is 0. If \(x\) is – 6, \((-6-1)(-6+6)\) gives (-7)(0), which is 0.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> \(x\)-intercepts of this graph.
    
    <figure><media alt="A curve in an x y plane, origin O, with grid. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 6, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 8 to 8, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 1 comma 0, 1 comma negative 4, and 3 comma 0."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6996d5bd6378e1a9f6b8fd7c0df4baa550e2ff48"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
  <list>
    <item> 1 </item>
    <item> -1 </item>
    <item> 3 </item>
    <item> -3 </item>
  </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -1, 3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the standard form of \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\) to identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>8</para></section>
</content>
